## Function components — the default

A component is a function that takes a props object and returns JSX:

```jsx
function Welcome({ name }) {
  return <h1>Hello, {name}</h1>;
}
```

An arrow function works identically. The name must be capitalised, and the function should be pure — same props and state, same output.

## Class components — the older form

```jsx
class Welcome extends React.Component {
  render() {
    return <h1>Hello, {this.props.name}</h1>;
  }
}
```

Still valid, but new code does not use them — hooks gave function components state and lifecycle with less ceremony.

## Composition over inheritance

You build bigger UIs by nesting components, not extending them:

```jsx
function App() {
  return (
    <Page>
      <Header />
      <Welcome name="Aaron" />
    </Page>
  );
}
```

## Keep them focused

A good component does one thing and is small enough to read at a glance. When a component juggles unrelated concerns, split it — and lift shared logic into a custom hook rather than a base class.

