## What hooks are

Hooks are functions that let a function component hook into React features. The core set: `useState` for state, `useEffect` for side effects, `useContext` for context, plus `useRef`, `useMemo`, `useReducer`, and `useCallback`.

```jsx
function Profile({ id }) {
  const [user, setUser] = useState(null);
  useEffect(() => {
    fetchUser(id).then(setUser);
  }, [id]);
  return user ? <h1>{user.name}</h1> : <p>Loading…</p>;
}
```

## Why they were a step change

Before hooks, only class components had state and lifecycle. Hooks brought those to functions and, more importantly, made stateful logic reusable: you extract a `useUser(id)` custom hook and share it, with no HOC wrapper and no extra nesting in the tree.

## The advantages

Related logic stays together instead of being split across `componentDidMount` and `componentWillUnmount`. There is no `this`, no constructor, no binding. Components get shorter and easier to test.

## The rules

Hooks must be called at the top level of a component, in the same order every render — never inside conditions or loops. That order is how React keeps each hook's state straight. The `eslint-plugin-react-hooks` rule enforces it.

